<p>British Prime Minister Keir Starmer is trying to head off a Labour leadership challenge after election results across England, Scotland and Wales prompted Labour lawmakers to call for him to resign or set a timetable for leaving office. POLITICO put Labour&#x27;s English council losses at almost 1,500; AllSides said the party lost more than 1,400 local representatives. Labour also lost power in Wales for the first time since the Senedd was created in 1999 and fell back in Scotland. Reform UK gained more than 1,300 seats across England, while the Greens also advanced. Starmer plans a Monday speech promising a larger response on growth, defence, Europe and energy. He is expected to say his government will be defined by rebuilding ties with Brussels and putting Britain &quot;at the heart of Europe&quot;. Starmer has said he will not resign and has described his government as a 10-year project, while some Labour MPs and former senior figures have said the party must change course after the results.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/eEmTgZE08oU4KA4KBy4lG0LFH_6ckuGMek7ba9HgV3dEaQA6MuLTdjHmH-9-J2GSy4aLBQlEuG6eu_FQsGKVqZuHvDaCB0dqRusqqkVUs9UptVtoVU-5r1zDRa3Ye-OG5kbLtUf6HVbcFudcwGSzZQW-GtW0nwE2VJBLVZ1vTuZkyGXpxbRCRqyNzcItBJJdpOFtYPgsk3c_GbwljYnczPQu-vNxCNsxHg3pxw' alt='Prime Minister Keir Starmer of Britain speaking to supporters and councilors in London on Friday, the day after elections were held in England, Wales and Scotland.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Internal pressure: The Guardian reported that about 40 Labour MPs had called on Starmer to quit as potential rivals began positioning themselves.</li><li>Leadership route: British parties can replace a prime minister without a general election if the governing party changes leader midterm.</li><li>Reset framing: Starmer is set to use the phrase &quot;strength through fairness&quot; and say the coming programme will show &quot;hope, urgency and exactly whose side we are on&quot;.</li><li>Cabinet stance: Education Secretary Bridget Phillipson said a leadership contest was not the answer, and Channel 4 reported that she insisted Starmer would lead Labour into the next general election.</li></ul><blockquote>Incremental change won&#x27;t cut it - Keir Starmer</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Keir Starmer: Starmer said the lesson from the vote was to listen to voters, but argued that did not mean simply &quot;tacking right or left.&quot; (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1999068/starmer-vows-to-listen-to-voters-after-election-drubbing'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Angela Rayner: Rayner said Labour’s current approach was not working, called the moment possibly the party’s last chance and backed Andy Burnham’s return to Westminster. (<a href='https://www.huffingtonpost.co.uk/entry/angela-rayner-tells-keir-starmer-to-change-course-now-as-pm-fights-to-save-his-job_uk_6a00a938e4b06e786e3ed425'>HuffPost UK</a>)</li><li>Catherine West: West said she would try to trigger a leadership contest if Starmer’s Monday speech did not satisfy her. (<a href='https://www.independent.com.mt/articles/2026-05-11/world-news/Britain-s-Starmer-fights-for-his-job-as-calls-for-his-ouster-grow-after-local-election-losses-6736289598'>The Malta Independent</a>)</li><li>Clive Betts: Betts said the Cabinet had a responsibility to speak to Starmer and that the party should set a timetable to bring in a new leader in a proper and constructive manner. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1999068/starmer-vows-to-listen-to-voters-after-election-drubbing'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>John Swinney: Scotland’s first minister called for an independence referendum so Scotland could protect itself against a possible future Reform UK government. (<a href='https://www.nettavisen.no/nyheter/keir-starmer-kjemper-for-jobben-varsler-kraftigere-skyts/s/5-95-3030153'>Nettavisen</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/keir-starmer-labour-party-leadership-chaos/'>Keir Starmer has one last chance 